<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Nutrition – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Nutrition</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Why Is Nutrition Important?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Create a narrative about a memorable event or occurrence. Interactive: Create responses to class survey questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a short narrative about a memorable meal with family, including traditional Panamanian foods (e.g., "Describe the special Sunday meal of your abuelita's sancocho and rice and explain why it's meaningful to your family.")
Can create responses to questions about dietary choices (e.g., "I often eat yogurt because it's healthy and has probiotics that help with digestion.")
Can respond to survey questions on food preferences (e.g., "I prefer fruits over other snacks because they are natural and provide more nutrients.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 40%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 10%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of various Panamanian dishes and international healthy food options (e.g., sancocho, arroz con pollo, fruits, vegetables, yogurt, processed snacks). Students are asked to quickly jot down in their notebooks 2-3 words or short phrases about what they *should* eat more of and why, or what they *prefer* to eat and its benefits, connecting to past lessons on nutrition. The goal is to activate prior vocabulary and grammar related to giving advice and justification.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing two sentences on the board. For example: "I prefer fruits over chips because they are natural and provide more vitamins." and "You should eat more whole grains if you want more energy." The teacher emphasizes the use of 'should' for advice and 'because' for justification. The teacher also models how to pronounce multisyllabic words like "vitamins" and "carbohydrates," focusing on correct stress.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'You should eat more vegetables,' am I telling you something good or bad to do?" (Expected answer: "Good.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'because,' am I giving a reason or asking a question?" (Expected answer: "Giving a reason.")</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher also asks students to repeat words like "nutrition" and "metabolism" to check pronunciation of stress.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Nutrition")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative text (approximately 100-120 words) describing a memorable family meal in Panama (e.g., a Sunday sancocho gathering). The text incorporates the target grammar: modal "should" (e.g., "Grandma always said we should eat all the vegetables."), first conditional (e.g., "If you eat well, you stay healthy and strong."), and adverbial clauses for justification (e.g., "Sancocho is a staple because it's nutritious and brings us together."). It also features key vocabulary like 'staple', 'nutritious', 'balanced diet', 'fiber', 'protein', 'benefits', 'digestion', and adjectives like 'fresh' and 'whole'. The text is displayed on the projector or as a handout.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students recognize and understand the use of narrative structures and descriptive language in a written text about nutrition and family experiences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the text individually.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main idea of this story about the family meal?" (e.g., "It's about a special family meal and its importance.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students answer T/F questions based on the text (e.g., "The family eats fast food every Sunday. T/F," "Grandma believes good nutrition is important. T/F").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match sentences from the text to the grammar structure used (e.g., Match a sentence with 'should' to "giving advice," a sentence with 'if...then' to "first conditional," or a sentence with 'because' to "justification").</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing sentences and short paragraphs using modal "should" for advice, first conditional for facts, and adverbial clauses (with "because") for justification. They will integrate target vocabulary related to nutrition and Panamanian food culture. Focus on stress in multisyllabic words like "digestion" and "carbohydrates" as they write and silently rehearse sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides sentence starters or cloze exercises in their notebooks. Students complete sentences using the target grammar and vocabulary.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You should eat more fruits __________." (because they provide vitamins)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you drink enough water, you __________." (will stay hydrated)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Good nutrition is important __________." (because it improves your health)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I prefer __________ over __________ because __________." (e.g., I prefer fresh vegetables over processed snacks because they are more beneficial for my body.)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Sentence Expansion/Combination):</b> Students are given simple facts or ideas and asked to combine them into more complex sentences using the target grammar.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Eating balanced meals. Stay healthy." -> "If you eat balanced meals, you will stay healthy."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Avoid processed foods. Unhealthy fats." -> "You should avoid processed foods because they often contain unhealthy fats."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Sancocho is a typical Panamanian dish. It has many nutrients." -> "Sancocho is a typical Panamanian dish because it has many nutrients."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Paragraph Writing & Peer Check:</b> In pairs, students create 2-3 sentences describing a healthy habit or food using "should" or "if" and justifying with "because." For example, "You should eat breakfast every day because it gives you energy for class. If you skip breakfast, you might feel tired." They then exchange notebooks with another pair for a quick peer check, verifying the correct use of "should," "if...then," and "because," and identifying new vocabulary words used.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Nutrition")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual Written Product.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> Students will write a short descriptive narrative (2-3 paragraphs, 60-80 words) in their notebooks about a memorable meal with their family, focusing on traditional Panamanian foods. They must describe the meal, explain why it is meaningful to their family, and incorporate advice or facts about nutrition related to the foods. They also need to integrate responses to two provided survey questions about dietary choices or preferences within their narrative or as separate justified responses.
                <br>
                <b>Writing Prompt:</b> "Describe a special family meal, perhaps a Sunday lunch with sancocho or arroz con pollo. What foods were eaten? Why is this meal meaningful to your family? In your writing, also respond to two of these questions:
                <br>
                1. What healthy food do you often eat and why?
                <br>
                2. Do you prefer fruits over other snacks? Why or why not?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> This task directly addresses the theme "Why Is Nutrition Important?" by having students reflect on the nutritional and cultural significance of food. It integrates sociolinguistic aspects by discussing healthy eating in the context of Panamanian family celebrations and allows pragmatic expression by offering advice or justifications within their writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in written expression, accurate use of target grammar (modal 'should', first conditional, adverbial clauses with 'because'), appropriate use of nutrition vocabulary, and completion of the communicative task (describing a meal, explaining meaning, and responding to survey questions with justification).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during writing and a simple rubric for evaluating the written product.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their written narratives and survey responses. The teacher quickly reviews them for content, grammar, and vocabulary usage.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Rubric - quick check):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Content (40%):</b> Narrative includes a memorable meal and its meaning; responds to at least one survey question with a justified answer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar (30%):</b> Correct use of "should," first conditional, and/or "because" for justification.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary (20%):</b> Uses at least 3-5 target vocabulary words (e.g., nutrition, healthy, benefits, staple, prefer, digest).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Organization (10%):</b> Ideas are understandable and somewhat organized in short paragraphs.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences at the bottom of their page before handing it in:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today was _________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Something I learned about writing advice or justifications today is _________."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing about nutrition was most challenging for you today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you use 'should' or 'because' to talk about healthy eating outside of class?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., clarity of prompts, engagement with topic, specific grammar practice)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration)? (e.g., more support for narrative structure, additional vocabulary review, explicit instruction on sentence connectors for more complex justifications)."</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5, students will use their knowledge of nutrition and their writing skills to mediate and integrate information, potentially by summarizing key nutritional advice or explaining Panamanian food traditions to someone from another culture, preparing for the 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>